# Regression test: a top-level (main-body) call site passing a struct-typed
# local -- specifically a struct-ELEMENT array (`[Item]`) here, though a bare
# struct local hits the same bug -- must still get auto-borrowed (`&local`),
# same as the identical call from inside an ordinary function body already
# did correctly.
#
# Root cause: `Transpiler::inferred_qualifiers` (a `HashMap<String, OwnerQual>`
# keyed by bare variable/parameter NAME, src/transpiler/mod.rs) is populated by
# `infer_qualifiers()` for each real function/method body in turn, but is never
# saved/restored around that per-function pass the way essentially every other
# per-function map is (`var_types`, `fn_current_params`, `resident_call_vars`,
# ...) -- see `emit_fn`'s (src/transpiler/emit_top.rs) many `std::mem::take`/
# restore pairs, none of which cover `inferred_qualifiers`. So after the LAST
# real function/method is transpiled, the map is simply left holding whatever
# THAT function computed for ITS OWN parameters.
#
# The synthesized top-level `fn main()` (leftover top-level statements, built
# by `Transpiler::emit_program_items`, src/transpiler/mod.rs) never ran
# `infer_qualifiers` over its own locals at all before this fix -- unlike a
# real function body, which always reruns it (implicitly clearing it) via
# `emit_body`. `emit_args_coerced`'s (src/transpiler/emit_methods.rs) call-site
# borrow decision consults `inferred_qualifiers.get(var_name)` BEFORE falling
# back to the variable's own declared type -- so if a top-level local happens
# to share a bare NAME with some other already-emitted function's own
# auto-ref-inferred `Borrow`/`BorrowMut` parameter (a natural collision:
# `items`, here, matches `check_all`'s own `[Item] items` parameter), the
# stale entry wins, and the required `&`/`&mut` is silently dropped at EVERY
# top-level call site using that name -- confirmed via a real `cargo build`
# E0308 ("expected `&Vec<Item>`, found `Vec<Item>`") on the emitted Rust.
# Reproduces identically for a struct-element array, a bare struct local, and
# a primitive-element array -- name collision is the only thing that matters,
# not the collection's element type.
#
# Fixed by re-running `infer_qualifiers` over the synthesized `main()`'s own
# top-level statements (`Item::Let` + `Item::Stmt`, combined into `Stmt`s)
# right before emitting them, exactly mirroring what `emit_body` already does
# first for every real function body -- this discards whatever the last
# function left behind and computes `items`'s OWN (correct) qualifier from how
# it's actually used at top level, instead of reusing someone else's.
#
# Only caught by `cargo run` on the emitted Rust (this test), never by
# `boring run`'s untyped interpreter (also registered in tests/run.rs, since
# the interpreter has always evaluated this correctly).

struct Item:
    int id

def check_all([Item] items):
    for item in items:
        print "{item.id}"

var mut [Item] items = []
items.push(Item(id = 1))
items.push(Item(id = 2))
check_all(items)
check_all(items)
